A||N||8||orystb||motor swap||||5||
Z||000000||orystb||11-06-2000||12:04 AM||||Has anyone tried to put a sho engine into a fiero? I got an engine at a good deal FREE. So if there is anyone out there that did this type of swap I would appreciate any help I can get on making this work.||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||11-06-2000||12:39 AM||||Bob Steiger at Fiero Parts Depot is doing one.  I saw it last month when I stopped by his shop.  It is a tight fit, but looks great.  It's not running yet, but he said it should be done by the first of the year.  <P>This isn't an easy swap to do, so it may not be something you'll want to do.||||reg||1
Z||000002||msweldon||11-06-2000||01:21 PM||||As wonderful as the Yamaha built V6 or V8 SHO engine is, and I researched the hell out of it for the fiero, the swap into a fiero was a bit more than I was willing to bite off(I'm just a weekend warrior mechanic).<P>This led me to look into the 3.4 DOHC swap.  Essentially its the same block as the 2.8, bolts to the fiero tranny, gets roughly the same performance as the SHO, same motor mounts, weighs slightly more than the stock 2.8 even with aluminum heads(really stout cam carriers), there are a few small computer, A/C, and speedometer issues but it is much more easily swapped than a SHO.<P>I'm in the middle of the purchasing phase for my swap, I'm actually trucking the DOHC engine to my house this week from a boneyard in FL.<P>So you may want to consider a 3.4 DOHC, just my .02 cents.<P>mw||||reg||1
Z||000003||orystb||11-06-2000||01:55 PM||||Its great to hear someone else out there is trying to attempt the same swap. I would like to know if you are using the trans that came with the engine or the fiero trans. Iam going to give it a try. Would you be willing to tell me how you have got the motor mounts set up in the fiero. Iam using the V6 sho motor with an automatic hooked to it. And again Iam thanking you for all the help that you may be able to give me. I think this list is GREAT.||||reg||14
Z||000004||Black88GT||11-06-2000||02:05 PM||||The 3.4 DOHC is a totally different engine.  Teh 3.4 OHV is a 60 degree V6 that is essentially the same block as the 2.8/3.1.  The 3.4 DOHC is about as hard to do as a 3.8, i.e. the reason why most people do the 3.8.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000005||3400Fiero||11-06-2000||02:32 PM||||The 3400 DOHC swap is easier to do than a 3800 swap if you want to run a manual transmission because the 3800 will require a custom flywheel.  If you are using an auto, it probably is as hard as a 3800.<P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC<BR>||||reg||1
